    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The subject-matter of this master's thesis focuses on perforated paper bands wound up on spools known as music rolls. They were used in the first third of the 20th century as tone production carriers of then widespread mechanical music instruments. The master's thesis consists of three main parts. The first part is dedicated to the external and manufacturer related characteristics of music rolls based on a collection of twelve German and American products. Each characteristic is evaluated for its suitability in identifying this category of objects by manufacturer, brand, and instrument. The second part highlights the materiality of the music roll papers developed for technical purposes in the course of the industrialization. An approach to these so far largely unexplored specialty papers is carried out in a first step through the consideration of related data storage mediums, such as the Jacquard board. Furthermore, the material-specific properties of music rolls and how these are technologically achieved is examined. In the third part, the comparison of the conservation and restoration versus the digitalization of music rolls for safeguarding the data is made. This then introduces issues in the conservation, handling, storage as well as damage categories of music rolls.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Autorin: Yasmine Kerber ; Referent: Prof. Mag. art akad. Rest. Sebastian Dobrusskin ; Koreferent: Prof. Mag. art. M. Mus. Martin Skamletz",
            "type": "statement of responsibility",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
